{"results":[{"url":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/judge-scolds-doj-over-case-against-ex-fbi-officials-accused-of-false-statements-about-hunter-bidens-laptop","final_url":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/judge-scolds-doj-over-case-against-ex-fbi-officials-accused-of-false-statements-about-hunter-bidens-laptop","title":"Judge scolds DOJ over case against ex-FBI officials accused of false statements about Hunter Biden's laptop","description":"A federal judge has scolded the Justice Department for its unorthodox handling of a criminal case against two former FBI officials.","language":"en","text":"By — Eric Tucker, Associated Press Eric Tucker, Associated Press By — Michael Kunzelman, Associated Press Michael Kunzelman, Associated Press Leave your feedback Share Copy URL https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/judge-scolds-doj-over-case-against-ex-fbi-officials-accused-of-false-statements-about-hunter-bidens-laptop Email Facebook Twitter LinkedIn Pinterest Tumblr Share on Facebook Share on Twitter Judge scolds DOJ over case against ex-FBI officials accused of false statements about Hunter Biden's laptop Nation Oct 27, 2026 1:34 PM EST WASHINGTON (AP) — A federal judge on Tuesday scolded the Justice Department for its unorthodox handling of a criminal case against two former FBI officials accused of making false statements to Congress about their participation in and knowledge of investigations involving Hunter Biden, the son of former President Joe Biden. U.S. Magistrate Zia Faruqui said he was distressed that prosecutors overnight filed an amended criminal complaint against Nikki Floris and Bradley Benavides that included a new obstruction charge — even though such charging documents require sign-off from a judge that probable cause exists. \"It is not our court's practice, and I think it is appropriately not our practice, that unsigned complaints are filed on the docket,\" Faruqui said. The judge was weighing a request from defense lawyers to strike the complaint, though if that were to happen, the Justice Department could still try to seek a felony indictment to push the case forward. The prosecution is the latest in a series of splashy and politically charged investigative actions the Justice Department has undertaken in the run-up to next week's midterm elections. The case says officials falsely minimized what they knew The case accuses the former officials of falsely playing down their knowledge about a laptop the Justice Department says Hunter Biden abandoned at a computer repair shop in 2019. But beyond that, it represents a Trump administration broadside against a since-disbanded FBI task force that was set up to monitor foreign influence. Congressional Republicans have long criticized the task force, saying it tried to discredit their own investigations into the Biden family. Defense lawyers were set to challenge the case at the preliminary hearing Tuesday in Washington's federal court, including by highlighting the unusual fact that the ex-FBI officials were charged via a criminal complaint signed by a magistrate judge rather than an indictment voted on by a grand jury. The Justice Department has repeatedly struggled in high-profile cases to persuade grand juries in the nation's capital to return indictments. Hours before the hearing, the Justice Department added a new obstruction charge that said the former officials' alleged false statements allowed them to avoid questioning about their knowledge of Hunter Biden and impeded an investigation that House lawmakers were doing into the Bidens. \"What is going on at the D.C. U.S. Attorney's Office?\" lawyers for Floris asked in a statement early Tuesday. \"In the middle of the night, the government filed an unsigned and unsworn complaint attempting to fix the issues with their original charge against Ms. Floris and adding a new charge just as meritless as the first one.\" \"In our combined decades of DOJ service,\" the lawyers said, \"we have never seen or even heard of something like this being filed.\" It is a very intricate saga The prosecution, a rare Justice Department criminal case against a former employee, revisits a tangled saga that roiled the final weeks of the 2020 race after the New York Post revealed the existence of a laptop it said had been dropped off by the younger Biden at a Delaware computer repair shop about a year earlier. The Post said it had obtained a hard drive of the laptop from Rudy Giuliani, a close ally of President Donald Trump, and reported on some of the contents of the computer, including emails that appeared to show some of Hunter Biden's foreign business dealings. READ MORE: 2 ex-FBI officials charged with making false statements about Hunter Biden's laptop The curious circumstances of the laptop's discovery raised concerns among Democrats and former intelligence officials about the possibility of Russian involvement, particularly when the Trump administration's own intelligence community had assessed that Moscow was attempting to undermine the candidacy of Democratic nominee Joe Biden, who went on to defeat Trump in 2020, and as Giuliani had traveled abroad looking for dirt on the Bidens. But Trump's then-national intelligence director, John Ratcliffe, quickly sought to quell speculation about the laptop's provenance by saying there were no signs it had anything to do with Russian actors. The FBI said it had nothing to add to Ratcliffe's statement and investigated the younger Biden on gun and tax charges that later resulted in felony convictions. President Joe Biden pardoned his son in late 2024. The accused led the Foreign Influence Task Force The FBI officials under scrutiny, Floris and Benavides, were leaders of the Foreign Influence Task Force, which was established in the first Trump administration to police influence campaigns staged by Russia and other nations aimed at sowing discord, undermining democracy and spreading disinformation. In that capacity, the FBI officials briefed Republican Sens. Chuck Grassley and Ron Johnson in August 2020 as the lawmakers were doing their own investigation into Hunter Biden's work in Ukraine. News of the briefing leaked, and the senators lashed out at the FBI, saying their efforts to scrutinize the Biden family had been discredited and falsely linked to Russian disinformation. The criminal complaint accuses them of lying to lawmakers in 2023 by denying contemporaneous knowledge of the Biden laptop or participating in internal FBI conversations about it. Their lawyers have denied any allegations of wrongdoing. The task force was disbanded in February 2025 by then-Attorney General Pam Bondi. A free press is a cornerstone of a healthy democracy. Support trusted journalism and civil dialogue. Donate now By — Eric Tucker, Associated Press Eric Tucker, Associated Press By — Michael Kunzelman, Associated Press Michael Kunzelman, Associated Press\n\nWASHINGTON (AP) — A federal judge on Tuesday scolded the Justice Department for its unorthodox handling of a criminal case against two former FBI officials accused of making false statements to Congress about their participation in and knowledge of investigations involving Hunter Biden, the son of former President Joe Biden. U.S. Magistrate Zia Faruqui said he was distressed that prosecutors overnight filed an amended criminal complaint against Nikki Floris and Bradley Benavides that included a new obstruction charge — even though such charging documents require sign-off from a judge that probable cause exists. \"It is not our court's practice, and I think it is appropriately not our practice, that unsigned complaints are filed on the docket,\" Faruqui said. The judge was weighing a request from defense lawyers to strike the complaint, though if that were to happen, the Justice Department could still try to seek a felony indictment to push the case forward. The prosecution is the latest in a series of splashy and politically charged investigative actions the Justice Department has undertaken in the run-up to next week's midterm elections. The case says officials falsely minimized what they knew The case accuses the former officials of falsely playing down their knowledge about a laptop the Justice Department says Hunter Biden abandoned at a computer repair shop in 2019. But beyond that, it represents a Trump administration broadside against a since-disbanded FBI task force that was set up to monitor foreign influence. Congressional Republicans have long criticized the task force, saying it tried to discredit their own investigations into the Biden family. Defense lawyers were set to challenge the case at the preliminary hearing Tuesday in Washington's federal court, including by highlighting the unusual fact that the ex-FBI officials were charged via a criminal complaint signed by a magistrate judge rather than an indictment voted on by a grand jury. The Justice Department has repeatedly struggled in high-profile cases to persuade grand juries in the nation's capital to return indictments. Hours before the hearing, the Justice Department added a new obstruction charge that said the former officials' alleged false statements allowed them to avoid questioning about their knowledge of Hunter Biden and impeded an investigation that House lawmakers were doing into the Bidens. \"What is going on at the D.C. U.S. Attorney's Office?\" lawyers for Floris asked in a statement early Tuesday. \"In the middle of the night, the government filed an unsigned and unsworn complaint attempting to fix the issues with their original charge against Ms. Floris and adding a new charge just as meritless as the first one.\" \"In our combined decades of DOJ service,\" the lawyers said, \"we have never seen or even heard of something like this being filed.\" It is a very intricate saga The prosecution, a rare Justice Department criminal case against a former employee, revisits a tangled saga that roiled the final weeks of the 2020 race after the New York Post revealed the existence of a laptop it said had been dropped off by the younger Biden at a Delaware computer repair shop about a year earlier. The Post said it had obtained a hard drive of the laptop from Rudy Giuliani, a close ally of President Donald Trump, and reported on some of the contents of the computer, including emails that appeared to show some of Hunter Biden's foreign business dealings. READ MORE: 2 ex-FBI officials charged with making false statements about Hunter Biden's laptop The curious circumstances of the laptop's discovery raised concerns among Democrats and former intelligence officials about the possibility of Russian involvement, particularly when the Trump administration's own intelligence community had assessed that Moscow was attempting to undermine the candidacy of Democratic nominee Joe Biden, who went on to defeat Trump in 2020, and as Giuliani had traveled abroad looking for dirt on the Bidens. But Trump's then-national intelligence director, John Ratcliffe, quickly sought to quell speculation about the laptop's provenance by saying there were no signs it had anything to do with Russian actors. The FBI said it had nothing to add to Ratcliffe's statement and investigated the younger Biden on gun and tax charges that later resulted in felony convictions. President Joe Biden pardoned his son in late 2024. The accused led the Foreign Influence Task Force The FBI officials under scrutiny, Floris and Benavides, were leaders of the Foreign Influence Task Force, which was established in the first Trump administration to police influence campaigns staged by Russia and other nations aimed at sowing discord, undermining democracy and spreading disinformation. In that capacity, the FBI officials briefed Republican Sens. Chuck Grassley and Ron Johnson in August 2020 as the lawmakers were doing their own investigation into Hunter Biden's work in Ukraine. News of the briefing leaked, and the senators lashed out at the FBI, saying their efforts to scrutinize the Biden family had been discredited and falsely linked to Russian disinformation. The criminal complaint accuses them of lying to lawmakers in 2023 by denying contemporaneous knowledge of the Biden laptop or participating in internal FBI conversations about it. Their lawyers have denied any allegations of wrongdoing. The task force was disbanded in February 2025 by then-Attorney General Pam Bondi. A free press is a cornerstone of a healthy democracy. Support trusted journalism and civil dialogue. Donate now","author":null,"published_date":"2026-10-27T13:34:26-05:00","latency_ms":1982.3389320008573,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}